<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/1st-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Revolt</p><h1 class="enc-title">Boudican revolt</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">60–61</span> · <a href="/centuries/1st-century">1st century</a> · Also known as Boudica's revolt, Revolt of Boudica</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Boudican revolt was an uprising of the Iceni and neighbouring peoples of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> against Roman rule in 60 or 61 CE, led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boudica">Boudica</a>, widow of the Iceni king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prasutagus">Prasutagus</a>. Provoked by the seizure of the Iceni kingdom and the abuse of Boudica and her daughters by Roman officials, the rebels destroyed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/camulodunum">Camulodunum</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/londinium">Londinium</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/verulamium">Verulamium</a>, killing, according to Roman writers, some 70,000 to 80,000 people. The governor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaius-suetonius-paulinus">Gaius Suetonius Paulinus</a> defeated them in a decisive battle, after which Roman rule in the province was firmly re-established.</p></div>
